Nexus Data Pipeline Documentation
Overview
The Nexus data pipeline transforms the Global Forest Watch Forest Carbon Monitoring Dataset from Excel format into a structured SQLite database optimized for natural language queries. The system processes approximately 37,000 rows of forest monitoring data spanning 165 countries and 24 years into three specialized fact tables.
Data Loading Process
Source Data Structure
The pipeline ingests data from a single Excel file (global_05212025.xlsx) containing three critical worksheets:

Country tree cover loss - Contains deforestation data for all 165 countries across 8 tree canopy density thresholds (0%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 50%, 75%) from 2001-2024
Country primary loss - Contains virgin/old-growth forest loss data exclusively for 75 tropical countries at a fixed 30% threshold from 2002-2023
Country carbon data - Contains carbon emissions and sequestration data for all countries but only at three thresholds (30%, 50%, 75%) from 2001-2024

Loading Mechanism
The loader uses Polars DataFrame library for memory-efficient processing. Each Excel sheet is loaded into memory with automatic caching to avoid redundant file reads. The loader validates that all required sheets exist before proceeding and maintains a registry of loaded data for performance optimization.
Data Transformation Architecture
Three-Table Design Philosophy
The transformation process deliberately creates three separate fact tables instead of a unified sparse matrix. This critical design decision avoids the ~60% NULL values that would result from a single table approach, as primary forest data only exists for tropical countries and carbon data only exists at specific thresholds.
Transformation Process
Wide-to-Long Format Conversion
The Excel data arrives in "wide" format with separate columns for each year (e.g., tc_loss_ha_2001, tc_loss_ha_2002). The transformers convert this to "long" format where year becomes a data dimension, creating one row per country-year-threshold combination.
Tree Cover Loss Transformation

Input: ~1,320 rows (165 countries × 8 thresholds)
Output: ~31,680 rows (165 countries × 24 years × 8 thresholds)
Computed Fields:

Loss rate percentage (loss divided by 2000 extent)
Data quality flags (NULL, ZERO, VALID, INVALID)



Primary Forest Transformation

Input: ~75 rows (tropical countries only)
Output: ~1,725 rows (75 countries × 23 years)
Special Rules:

Threshold always fixed at 30% (FAO standard)
Tropical country flag added
Loss status categorization (NO_DATA, NO_LOSS, LOSS_RECORDED)



Carbon Data Transformation

Input: ~495 rows (165 countries × 3 thresholds)
Output: ~11,880 rows (165 countries × 24 years × 3 thresholds)
Special Processing:

Only processes thresholds 30%, 50%, and 75%
Carbon flux status determination (SINK if negative, SOURCE if positive)
Preserves negative values for net flux (indicates carbon absorption)



Data Validation System
Multi-Level Validation
Structural Validation

Verifies all required columns exist
Confirms year columns follow expected patterns (YYYY format)
Validates data types (numeric columns are actually numeric)

Business Rule Validation

Tropical Country Constraint: Primary forest data must only contain the 75 designated tropical countries
Threshold Constraints: Carbon data must only have 30%, 50%, or 75% thresholds
Year Range Validation:

Tree cover: 2001-2024
Primary forest: 2002-2023 (starts one year later)
Carbon: 2001-2024



Data Quality Validation

Completeness Check: Ensures >70% non-null values in numeric columns
Negative Value Detection: Flags inappropriate negative values (except for carbon net flux)
Relationship Validation: Ensures primary forest loss never exceeds total tree cover loss for the same country-year

Cleaning Operations

Country name standardization (e.g., "USA" → "United States")
Whitespace trimming
Impossible value capping (loss cannot exceed forest extent)
NULL value handling strategies

SQLite Database Structure
Database Location
data/processed/forest.db
Fact Tables
fact_tree_cover_loss

Primary Key: (country, year, threshold)
Columns: country, year, threshold, tree_cover_loss_ha, extent_2000_ha, extent_2010_ha, area_ha, loss_rate_pct, data_quality_flag
Row Count: ~31,680
Indexes: On country-year, threshold, and year for query optimization

fact_primary_forest

Primary Key: (country, year)
Columns: country, year, threshold (always 30), primary_forest_loss_ha, is_tropical, loss_status
Row Count: ~1,725
Indexes: On country-year and is_tropical flag

fact_carbon

Primary Key: (country, year, threshold)
Columns: country, year, threshold, carbon_emissions_mg_co2e, carbon_emissions_annual_avg, carbon_removals_annual_avg, carbon_net_flux_annual_avg, carbon_density_mg_c_ha, carbon_flux_status
Row Count: ~11,880
Indexes: On country-year-threshold and carbon_flux_status

Dimension Tables

dim_location: Country reference with tropical flag
dim_time: Year dimension with decade and period categorizations

Optimized Views

v_primary_forest_percentage: Pre-calculated primary vs total forest loss percentages
v_carbon_intensity: Carbon emissions per hectare of forest lost
v_annual_summary: Yearly aggregates for quick trend analysis

Metadata System
Semantic Metadata (Version Controlled)
Stored in JSON files under config/semantic/:

mappings.json: Natural language to column name mappings
patterns.json: Query pattern recognition rules
rules.json: Business logic and constraints
constants.json: Domain constants (tropical countries list, valid thresholds)

Runtime Metadata (Generated)
Created during pipeline execution and stored in config/runtime/:

Actual row counts per table
Data completeness scores
Processing timestamps
Validation results
Data quality metrics

Database Connection and Querying
Connecting to the Database
Using SQLite command line:
bashsqlite3 data/processed/forest.db
Using Python:
bashuv run python
>>> import sqlite3
>>> conn = sqlite3.connect('data/processed/forest.db')
>>> cursor = conn.cursor()
Sample Queries
Basic forest loss query:
sqlSELECT country, year, tree_cover_loss_ha 
FROM fact_tree_cover_loss 
WHERE country = 'Brazil' AND year = 2023 AND threshold = 30;
Primary forest percentage:
sqlSELECT t.country, t.year,
       (p.primary_forest_loss_ha / t.tree_cover_loss_ha) * 100 as primary_pct
FROM fact_tree_cover_loss t
LEFT JOIN fact_primary_forest p 
  ON t.country = p.country AND t.year = p.year
WHERE t.threshold = 30 AND t.country = 'Brazil';
Carbon intensity calculation:
sqlSELECT c.carbon_emissions_mg_co2e / t.tree_cover_loss_ha as carbon_per_hectare
FROM fact_tree_cover_loss t
JOIN fact_carbon c 
  ON t.country = c.country AND t.year = c.year AND t.threshold = c.threshold
WHERE t.country = 'Indonesia' AND t.year = 2023;
Testing Strategy
Unit Testing Approach
Transformer Tests
Tests each transformer in isolation with small sample datasets (3-9 rows) to verify:

Correct wide-to-long format conversion
Proper year extraction from column names
Accurate computed field calculations
Data quality flag assignment

Cleaner Tests
Validates data cleaning operations:

Country name standardization
Negative value handling
Impossible value capping
NULL value processing

Validator Tests
Ensures business rules are enforced:

Tropical country verification for primary forest
Carbon threshold restrictions (30/50/75 only)
Year range constraints
Cross-table relationship validation

Testing Commands
Run all data pipeline tests:
bashuv run pytest tests/unit/data/ -v
Run specific test categories:
bash# Transformer tests only
uv run pytest tests/unit/data/test_transformers.py -v

# Validator tests only
uv run pytest tests/unit/data/test_validators.py -v

# With coverage report
uv run pytest tests/unit/data/ -v --cov=nexus.data.pipeline --cov-report=term-missing
